WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Security assumptions are beliefs about how the system is protected or operated.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:19.720
They may involve authentication, authorization, network exposure, encryption, secrets handling, logging, monitoring, backup and recovery, dependency ownership, third-party responsibility, and environment separation.

3
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.740
Some assumptions are confirmed.

4
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.680
Others are inherited from old decisions, tribal knowledge, or incomplete documentation.

5
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.480
Assumptions should be visible, reviewable, and testable.

6
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.380
A statement such as all admin actions require MFA is security-relevant.

7
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:46.160
So is production databases are not reachable from the public internet, secrets are managed by the platform service, or vendors do not receive regulated data.

8
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.980
If those statements are false or only partly true, the risk picture changes.

9
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:59.020
AI can help extract assumptions from text, diagrams, comments, and architecture notes.

10
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.400
It can also identify where a statement sounds like an assumption rather than confirmed evidence.

11
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.140
For example, words such as should, expected, normally, or assumed may signal uncertainty.

12
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:18.360
The output should label whether each assumption is confirmed, unverified, contradicted, or open for review.

13
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.000
Environment differences deserve special attention.

14
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:31.620
Development, test, staging, and production may have different data, identities, network rules, logging, and deployment practices.

15
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.920
An assumption that is true in production may be false in a lower environment.

16
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:44.140
Attack surface documentation should not collapse those environments into one generic description unless the evidence supports it.

17
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.820
Assumption tracking turns documentation into a security review tool.

18
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.400
Instead of hiding uncertainty, it gives teams a list of claims to validate.

19
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.100
That list can feed architecture review, control testing, backlog items, and future detection work.
